{"task_id": "HumanEval/41", "solution": "class CollisionResult:\n    \"\"\"A data holder for the number of collisions.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, collisions: int):\n        self.collisions = collisions\nclass InputValidator:\n    \"\"\"Validates input parameters for type and value.\"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def validateInteger(n):\n        if not isinstance(n, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def validateNonNegative(n):\n        if n < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\nclass CollisionCalculator:\n    \"\"\"Computes the number of collisions based on the problem logic.\"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def computeCollisions(n: int) -> CollisionResult:\n        collisions = n * n\n        return CollisionResult(collisions)\ndef car_race_collision(n: int):\n    \"\"\"\n    Imagine a road that's a perfectly straight infinitely long line.\n    n cars are driving left to right;  simultaneously, a different set of n cars\n    are driving right to left.   The two sets of cars start out being very far from\n    each other.  All cars move in the same speed.  Two cars are said to collide\n    when a car that's moving left to right hits a car that's moving right to left.\n    However, the cars are infinitely sturdy and strong; as a result, they continue moving\n    in their trajectory as if they did not collide.\n\n    This function outputs the number of such collisions.\n    \"\"\"\n    InputValidator.validateInteger(n)\n    InputValidator.validateNonNegative(n)\n    result = CollisionCalculator.computeCollisions(n)\n    return result.collisions"}
